Title:
Entropia

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- Entropia is a compiled programming language and toolchain for producing Windows x86-64 position-independent shellcode blobs and Cobalt Strike Beacon Object Files (BOFs) directly from `.etpy` source.  
- It replaces the typical multi-step offensive build pipeline (C compiler + linker + PIC transform like sRDI/Donut + custom OPSEC patching) with a single compiler that emits the final artifact without post-processing.  
- The project emphasizes “compiler-owned OPSEC” via flags/modules: polymorphic instruction rewriting, NOP padding, string obfuscation/stack strings, runtime import resolution (hashed imports), and direct/indirect syscall stubs.  
- It supports inline assembly (e.g., PEB walks), Win32 API calling via generated typed headers (`use_c`), and BOF ergonomics (Beacon API stubs, local `bof-runner`, and VS Code debugging via DAP).  
- Useful primarily for red teams, pentesters, and offensive tool developers who want repeatable, low-friction generation of evasive Windows payload components.  
- Interesting because it integrates multiple evasion and artifact-shaping techniques into the compiler IR, enabling per-build randomized diversity (reported 67–78% byte-level differences) and reducing “glue code” complexity.

Technical Focus:
- BOF (COFF) generation and Beacon API integration
- Position-independent shellcode compilation (Windows x64)
- Runtime API resolution via PEB walk / LoadLibraryA / GetProcAddress (hashed imports)
- Direct and indirect syscalls (HellsGate-style and syscall-gadget dispatch)
- Polymorphism and artifact randomization (instruction rewriting, NOP sleds, seeds)
- Pre-flight OPSEC modules (ETW/AMSI patching, NTDLL unhooking, sleep masks, stack spoofing)

Use Cases:
- Build Cobalt Strike BOFs without manual COFF/IAT/relocation handling
- Generate PIC shellcode for custom loaders or implant stages
- Rapid prototyping of Windows tradecraft (process enum, injection helpers, token ops) with typed Win32 bindings
- Produce per-build polymorphic artifacts to reduce signature stability
- Local testing/debugging of BOFs with a harness and VS Code breakpoints

Keywords:
